From 76c5a9b8f715256e3c2cf212faad847de15f2913 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 10:12:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] USB audio: the Deluge's output as a USB audio input on the
 computer

Community feature "USB audio" (off by default, read at boot). When on
and the Deluge starts as a USB peripheral, it enumerates as a USB Audio
Class 1.0 device next to its usual MIDI interface: stereo, 24 bits,
44.1 kHz, isochronous IN endpoint 3 on PIPE1, asynchronous, so the
computer records exactly what goes to the Deluge's outputs (the same
signal the Deluge records when resampling).

- io/usb/usb_audio_descriptor.c: device descriptor with release 2.01,
  configuration AudioControl (input terminal -> USB streaming, no
  feature unit, so no class requests) + the MIDI interface byte for
  byte + AudioStreaming (alt 0 / alt 1). Pipe table: the MIDI pipes as
  always, then PIPE1 (double buffered, FIFO blocks 24-33). Without the
  feature, the MIDI-only descriptors and pipe table are used unchanged.
- io/usb/usb_audio_stream.h: ring buffer between the audio engine and
  the USB interrupt, 24-bit packing, packet sizes 44/45 frames nudged
  by the smoothed fill level (asynchronous endpoint, no feedback
  endpoint needed), resync after the computer stopped reading, silence
  instead of clicks after the engine stalled.
- io/usb/usb_audio.cpp, usb_audio_pipe.c: priming both halves of the
  pipe, then refilling every free half on each "buffer free" interrupt
  through D0FIFO, which nothing else uses (MIDI uses CFIFO from the main
  loop). Tracks are rendered with interrupts off, so after a long one
  both halves may need refilling at once. Empty packets the computer
  got (NRDY on PIPE1) are counted and shown as "USB audio gap", at most
  every 10 seconds.
- USB driver: the interrupt handles PIPE1 itself and never queues its
  events (the queue handler takes every BRDY/BEMP for MIDI). With audio
  on, only BRDY bits of pipes whose interrupt is enabled go on to the
  queue, since stale bits of the MIDI send pipe or the DCP would now
  arrive every millisecond and stop MIDI reception. SET_INTERFACE only
  touches the interface that changes (the Renesas code forgot the other
  interfaces' endpoints and re-initialised every pipe); the MIDI
  interface still gets its pipes reset as before. SET_CONFIGURATION,
  bus reset and detach stop the stream.
- deluge.cpp: community features are read before USB starts.
- audio_engine.cpp: the frames sent to the codec also go to the ring,
  except during an offline stem export.

